-- title:
--   Proposition 3 — every admissible audit satisfies $\mathbb E_qC\ge\mu(a,d)\,\mathbb E_qN$
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be any audit rule (adaptive, possibly aborting and restarting, using old data and private randomness with law $\nu$), at depth $d\ge0$ with survival probability $a\in(0,1]$, and let $q\in[0,1]$. Then
--
--   $$
--   \mathbb E_qC\ \ge\ \mu(a,d)\,\mathbb E_qN,\qquad \mu(a,d)=\frac{1+\sum_{j=0}^{d}a^j}{a^d},
--   $$
--
--   where $C$ counts root setups, navigation attempts and target attempts and $N$ counts target observations; infinite expectations are allowed.
--
--   Each target observation must be preceded by a root setup and $d$ successful navigation transitions, and aborting can discard arrivals but cannot create them. This converts the information lower bound on $\mathbb E N$ into a lower bound on physical cost.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Both sides are in $[0,\infty]$.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: navigation and target outcomes with their stated conditional laws given the history; old transcript generated by a common kernel under all compared parameters; private randomness → side variable w ∼ ν (old data and private randomness, law free of q), independent of an i.i.d. Bernoulli(a) navigation tape and an i.i.d. Bernoulli(q) target tape; the k-th navigation attempt reads navigation entry k, the j-th target attempt reads target entry j (lawQ); reason: attempts are predictable, so each reads a fresh entry independent of everything observed; the joint law of (old data, randomness, fresh history) is the source's
--   - encoding: C the new physical cost (root setups, navigation attempts, target attempts), N the number of target observations, expectations E_q → cost and targetCount valued in ℕ∞, expectations as lintegrals in ℝ≥0∞ (expCost, expTargets); reason: same quantities; a rule that never stops has cost ⊤ and never accepts
--   - encoding: an auditor may adaptively abort or restart; root resets allowed, internal resets not → actions setup / attempt / accept / stop; setup issued during an episode abandons it; attempt with no active episode acts as a root setup (cost 1); decisions are measurable in w for each fresh history; reason: every abort/restart is a root setup; no action yields a free observation; measurability makes the acceptance events and costs well defined
-- source:
--   Q. Li, When Can Physical Evidence Survive a World-Model Update? Finite-cost re-auditing and reusable policy certificates, review revision of 3 November 2026 (STOC 2027 submission, sha256 a20c437b34115b84982339e9a6fa319cb3a66f66d9d4c474465cbb954c79093d), Proposition 3 (The physical navigation multiplier), first sentence, p.3 (PDF p.4)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_LimitedPriceChanges_LowerBound_KL
import Definitions.Def_PhysReaudit_mu
import Definitions.Def_PhysReaudit_Delta
import Definitions.Def_PhysReaudit_Rule
import Definitions.Def_PhysReaudit_run
import Definitions.Def_PhysReaudit_lawQ

open MeasureTheory

namespace PhysReaudit

/-- **Proposition 3, first sentence (the physical navigation multiplier)** (Q. Li, *When Can Physical Evidence Survive a World-Model Update?*, review revision of 3 November 2026 (sha256 a20c437b…), Proposition 3, p.3
(PDF p.4)): "Every admissible audit satisfies EqC ≥ µ(a, d)EqN."

For every side law `ν`, every rule `R`, every depth `d`, survival probability `a ∈ (0, 1]` and target
parameter `q ∈ [0, 1]`: `E_q C ≥ µ(a, d) · E_q N`, in `ℝ≥0∞` (infinite expectations allowed). -/
theorem proposition_3_lower {W : Type} [MeasurableSpace W] (ν : Measure W) [IsProbabilityMeasure ν]
    (R : Rule W) {a : ℝ} {d : ℕ} (ha : 0 < a) (ha1 : a ≤ 1) {q : ℝ} (hq : q ∈ Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) 1) :
    ENNReal.ofReal (mu a d) * expTargets R ν a d q ≤ expCost R ν a d q := by sorry

end PhysReaudit
